-- Q1: medium / L2 — UCC Article 2 firm offer (3-month cap)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Barnett Office Supply, a merchant, sends a signed, written offer on March 1, 2026, to Clearwater Inc. to sell 500 cases of paper at $28 per case, stating the offer will remain open for 120 days. On May 15, Barnett attempts to revoke the offer. Under UCC Article 2, how long is this firm offer irrevocable?', '["30 days, the default holding period for non-merchant offers", "120 days, the full period stated in the offer", "90 days, the maximum period for a merchant''s firm offer", "60 days, the standard period for commercial offers between merchants"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under UCC Section 2-205, a firm offer by a merchant that is in writing and signed is irrevocable for the stated period, but no longer than three months (90 days) without consideration. Barnett''s 120-day offer is therefore capped at 90 days. Choice B is incorrect because the UCC limits firm offers to three months regardless of the stated duration — any period beyond 90 days requires separate consideration to remain irrevocable.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Regulation D 506(b) vs 506(c) general solicitation tradeoff
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Vertex Technologies plans a $15 million private placement in 2026. The company wants to advertise broadly on social media to attract investors but also wants to include up to 20 non-accredited individuals who have investment experience. Vertex''s counsel explains that general solicitation and inclusion of non-accredited investors are mutually exclusive under Regulation D. Which exemption and investor limitation combination is available if Vertex chooses general solicitation?', '["Rule 506(b) permits general solicitation with up to 35 sophisticated non-accredited investors", "Rule 506(c) permits general solicitation but limits participation to accredited investors only", "Rule 504 permits general solicitation with unlimited non-accredited investors for offerings under $10 million", "Rule 506(c) permits general solicitation with up to 35 non-accredited investors if they are sophisticated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Securities Act Section 4(a)(2) and SEC Regulation D, Rule 506(c) permits general solicitation (including social media advertising) but requires that all purchasers be accredited investors whose status must be verified. Rule 506(b) allows up to 35 sophisticated non-accredited investors but prohibits general solicitation. Choice D is incorrect because Rule 506(c) does not permit any non-accredited investors regardless of their sophistication level.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — UCC Section 2-207 battle of the forms (additional terms between merchants)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Franklin Hardware orders 200 power drills from Piedmont Supply at $45 each. Piedmont sends an acknowledgment accepting the order but includes an additional term allowing 15 days for inspection of goods upon delivery. Franklin does not object to the inspection term. Under UCC Section 2-207, what is the status of Piedmont''s additional inspection term if both parties are merchants?', '["The inspection term is automatically rejected because it was not in the original offer", "The inspection term prevents contract formation until Franklin expressly agrees", "The inspection term is treated as a counteroffer requiring separate acceptance", "The inspection term becomes part of the contract because it does not materially alter the agreement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under UCC Section 2-207(2), between merchants, additional terms in an acceptance become part of the contract unless they materially alter the agreement, the offer expressly limits acceptance to its terms, or the offeror objects within a reasonable time. A reasonable inspection period is a standard commercial term that does not materially alter the agreement. Choice C is incorrect because Section 2-207 rejects the common law mirror image rule — an acceptance with additional terms is still an acceptance, not a counteroffer.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — UCC Article 2 scope (goods definition)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'UCC Article 2 governs transactions involving the sale of which type of property?', '["Movable, tangible personal property", "Real estate and fixtures", "Intangible property such as patents and copyrights", "Services and labor contracts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'UCC Article 2 applies exclusively to the sale of goods, defined as movable, tangible personal property. It does not apply to real estate, intangibles, or services. Choice B is incorrect because real estate transactions are governed by common law contract principles, not UCC Article 2.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — UCC Article 3 negotiable instrument requirements
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Under UCC Article 3, which of the following is a requirement for an instrument to be negotiable?', '["The instrument must be secured by collateral", "The instrument must contain an unconditional promise or order to pay a fixed amount of money", "The instrument must be payable only on a specific future date", "The instrument must be signed by both the maker and the payee"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under UCC Section 3-104, a negotiable instrument must be in writing, signed by the maker or drawer, contain an unconditional promise or order to pay a fixed amount of money, be payable on demand or at a definite time, and be payable to order or bearer. Choice A is incorrect because negotiable instruments need not be secured by collateral — they represent an independent payment obligation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Due diligence defense under Section 11
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Morrison, a partner at a public accounting firm, audits the financial statements included in Greenway Corp.''s registration statement filed under the Securities Act of 1933. After the offering, an investor discovers material misstatements in the financial statements. Morrison claims he conducted a reasonable investigation and had reasonable grounds to believe the statements were accurate. Under Section 11, which defense is Morrison asserting?', '["Contributory negligence defense", "Statute of limitations defense", "Due diligence defense", "Good faith reliance defense"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933, all parties except the issuer may assert the due diligence defense by proving they conducted a reasonable investigation and had reasonable grounds to believe the registration statement was accurate. Morrison, as a CPA (expert), may assert this defense for the audited financial statements. Choice D is incorrect because the good faith defense applies under Section 18 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, not Section 11.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Agency by estoppel
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Richmond allows Drake to use Richmond''s office, business cards, and company email to meet with clients for six months. Richmond never formally authorizes Drake as an agent, but Drake enters a contract with Patel, who reasonably believed Drake was Richmond''s authorized representative. Richmond now denies any agency relationship. Under the doctrine of estoppel, what is the most likely outcome?', '["Richmond is not bound because no formal agency agreement existed", "Patel has no recourse because Richmond never granted Drake actual authority", "Drake alone is liable because Drake acted without any authority", "Richmond is estopped from denying the agency because Patel relied on Richmond''s conduct to their detriment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under agency law, a principal who creates the appearance of an agency relationship through their own conduct is estopped from denying that agency when a third party has reasonably relied on that appearance to their detriment. Richmond''s provision of office space, business cards, and email created the appearance of authority. Choice A is incorrect because formal agreement is not required — the principal''s conduct and the third party''s reasonable reliance are sufficient to establish agency by estoppel.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Power of attorney
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Sullivan executes a document granting her financial advisor, Carter, the authority to manage all banking transactions, sign checks, and sell securities on her behalf. This document must be in writing and is often notarized for certain transactions. The document Sullivan executed is known as:', '["A power of attorney", "A revocable trust agreement", "A fiduciary appointment order", "A general authorization memorandum"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under agency law, a power of attorney is a written agency authorization that grants the agent (attorney-in-fact) authority to act on behalf of the principal. It must be in writing and may require notarization for transactions involving real property or financial accounts. Choice B is incorrect because a revocable trust creates a separate legal entity holding assets, whereas a power of attorney authorizes an agent to act on the principal''s behalf directly.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Perfect tender rule application
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Grayson Manufacturing orders 1,000 stainless steel bolts from Whitfield Supply, with delivery scheduled for April 15, 2026. On April 15, Whitfield delivers 1,000 bolts, but 12 bolts are slightly undersized and fail to meet contract specifications. Under UCC Article 2, what right does Grayson have regarding the shipment?', '["Grayson must accept the entire shipment because the defect is immaterial", "Grayson may reject the entire shipment because the goods fail to conform to the contract in any respect", "Grayson may reject only the 12 defective bolts and must accept the rest", "Grayson must negotiate a price reduction for the defective bolts"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under UCC Section 2-601 (the perfect tender rule), the buyer may reject the entire shipment, accept the entire shipment, or accept some commercial units and reject the rest if the goods fail to conform to the contract in any respect. Even 12 undersized bolts out of 1,000 justify rejection of the entire delivery. Choice A is incorrect because the perfect tender rule does not include a materiality threshold — any nonconformity entitles the buyer to reject.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L2 — Death terminating agency by operation of law
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Nelson appoints Shaw as his agent to manage rental properties. Nelson dies unexpectedly on June 10, 2026. Shaw is unaware of Nelson''s death and signs a lease with a new tenant on June 12. What is the effect of Nelson''s death on the agency and the lease?', '["The agency continues until Shaw receives formal notice of Nelson''s death", "The lease is valid because Shaw acted in good faith without knowledge of the death", "The agency terminates immediately upon Nelson''s death, and the lease is not binding on Nelson''s estate", "The agency is suspended but not terminated until probate proceedings conclude"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under common law agency principles, the death of either the principal or the agent immediately terminates the agency relationship by operation of law, without the need for notice. Any acts performed by the agent after the principal''s death are void and do not bind the principal''s estate. Choice B is incorrect because the agent''s good faith or lack of knowledge does not preserve authority that has terminated by operation of law.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — CPA liability: foreseen users (Restatement) vs Ultramares (near privity)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Graham & Associates CPAs negligently audit Delta Corp.''s financial statements. Pacific Investors, a venture capital firm that Delta''s management specifically identified to Graham as a potential investor, relies on the audit report and suffers a $3 million loss. Separately, Overland Bank, which Graham did not know would rely on the statements, also lends $1 million based on the same audit. Compare the outcomes under the Restatement (foreseen users) approach versus the Ultramares (near privity) doctrine.', '["Under the Restatement approach, Pacific Investors can recover because it was a foreseen user; under Ultramares, Pacific Investors cannot recover for ordinary negligence because it lacks privity with Graham", "Under both approaches, Pacific Investors can recover but Overland Bank cannot because it was not identified", "Under the Restatement approach, both can recover because any reliance is sufficient; under Ultramares, neither can recover", "Under Ultramares, Pacific Investors can recover because it was specifically identified; under the Restatement approach, only contractual clients can recover"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the Restatement (majority) approach, CPAs are liable to foreseen users and foreseen classes of users who rely on the CPA''s work. Pacific Investors was specifically identified to Graham and qualifies as a foreseen user. Under the Ultramares (minority) doctrine, CPA liability for ordinary negligence is limited to parties in privity or near privity — Pacific Investors lacks contractual privity with Graham and cannot recover for mere negligence. Choice B is incorrect because it ignores the Ultramares restriction on third-party negligence claims to those in privity.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: hard / L3 — Section 18 vs Rule 10b-5 defense comparison
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'An investor discovers that a publicly traded company filed a false Form 10-K with the SEC and separately made fraudulent statements in press releases that inflated the stock price. The investor purchased shares relying on both sources. Under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, the investor considers claims under both Section 18 and Rule 10b-5. Which key difference in the available defenses should the investor''s attorney analyze?', '["Section 18 requires proof of scienter while Rule 10b-5 does not", "Rule 10b-5 allows a due diligence defense while Section 18 does not", "Section 18 applies only to oral statements while Rule 10b-5 covers written filings", "Section 18 allows a good-faith defense while Rule 10b-5 requires proof of scienter"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, Section 18 imposes liability for false statements in documents filed with the SEC and allows defendants a good-faith defense. Rule 10b-5 is the broader antifraud provision requiring plaintiffs to prove scienter — intent to deceive or reckless disregard of the truth. Choice A is incorrect because it reverses the relationship: Rule 10b-5 requires scienter, whereas Section 18 requires only that the plaintiff prove reliance on the filed statement.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Registration process (waiting period activities)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Nova Pharmaceuticals files a registration statement with the SEC on February 1, 2026, for a public offering of common stock. During the waiting period before the registration becomes effective, Nova''s underwriter wants to generate investor interest. Under the Securities Act of 1933, what activities may the underwriter lawfully conduct during the waiting period?', '["Sell shares to qualified institutional buyers at a preliminary price", "Distribute a preliminary prospectus (red herring) but not sell securities", "Accept binding purchase commitments from accredited investors", "Issue a final prospectus and begin delivering shares to subscribers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the Securities Act of 1933, during the waiting period between filing the registration statement and its effective date, the issuer may distribute a preliminary prospectus (known as a red herring) to gauge investor interest but may not sell securities or accept binding commitments. Choice A is incorrect because no sales of registered securities are permitted until the registration statement becomes effective, regardless of investor qualification.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Issuer strict liability vs due diligence defense availability
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Pinnacle Energy Corp. files a registration statement containing a material misstatement in its financial projections. The CEO claims she conducted extensive due diligence and reasonably believed the projections were accurate. The underwriter makes the same claim. An investor who purchased shares at the offering price and suffered a loss brings suit under Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933. Evaluate the availability of the due diligence defense for each defendant.', '["Both the CEO and the underwriter may assert the due diligence defense because they conducted reasonable investigations", "Neither the CEO nor the underwriter may assert the defense because Section 11 imposes strict liability on all parties", "The issuer has strict liability with no due diligence defense, but the CEO and underwriter may each assert the defense if they conducted a reasonable investigation", "Only the underwriter may assert the defense because corporate officers share the issuer''s strict liability"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933, the issuer faces strict liability for material misstatements in the registration statement and cannot assert a due diligence defense. However, all other parties — including directors, signing officers (such as the CEO), underwriters, and experts — may avoid liability by proving they conducted a reasonable investigation and had reasonable grounds to believe the statement was accurate. Choice D is incorrect because corporate officers are distinct from the issuer entity and may individually assert the due diligence defense.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Predominant purpose test (mixed goods and services contract)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Archer Design Studio enters a $120,000 contract with Canton Corp. to design and install custom display fixtures. The contract allocates $85,000 to design and engineering services and $35,000 to the manufactured fixtures. A dispute arises over whether Canton can reject the fixtures for minor cosmetic imperfections. Evaluate which body of law governs and whether the perfect tender rule applies.', '["Common law governs because the predominant purpose is services, and Canton must prove a material breach to reject", "UCC Article 2 governs because the contract includes manufactured goods, and Canton may reject for any nonconformity", "Both common law and UCC apply simultaneously, with common law governing services and UCC governing the fixtures separately", "UCC Article 2 governs because manufactured fixtures are always classified as goods regardless of the service component"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the predominant purpose test, a mixed goods-and-services contract is governed entirely by whichever body of law corresponds to the contract''s predominant purpose. Because $85,000 (71%) of the $120,000 contract is allocated to services, common law governs the entire agreement. Under common law, the buyer must prove a material breach to justify rejection — the UCC perfect tender rule does not apply. Choice B is incorrect because the mere inclusion of goods does not trigger UCC Article 2 when services are the predominant purpose of the contract.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Regulation A exemption (simplified registration up to $75M)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Brightwell Corp., a startup, plans to raise $50 million from public investors in 2026 but wants to avoid the cost and complexity of full SEC registration. Brightwell''s counsel suggests using a simplified registration process. Under the Securities Act of 1933, which exemption allows offerings of up to $75 million with simplified disclosure requirements?', '["Rule 506(b) of Regulation D", "Rule 147 intrastate offering exemption", "Section 3(a)(11) bank securities exemption", "Regulation A offering exemption"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under the Securities Act of 1933, Regulation A provides a simplified registration process (sometimes called a mini-registration) for securities offerings up to $75 million. It allows public solicitation with reduced disclosure requirements compared to full registration. Choice A is incorrect because Rule 506(b) is a private placement exemption under Regulation D that prohibits general solicitation, not a simplified public registration process.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Proxy solicitation disclosure requirements
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Westfield Industries is preparing for its annual shareholder meeting in 2026. Management plans to solicit proxies from shareholders to vote on the election of directors and a proposed merger. Under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, what must Westfield provide to shareholders when soliciting proxies?', '["A copy of the company''s articles of incorporation and bylaws", "A proxy statement containing material information about the matters to be voted on", "A summary of all SEC enforcement actions taken against the company", "A binding commitment letter guaranteeing the outcome of the vote"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 14 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, any person soliciting proxies must furnish shareholders with a proxy statement containing material information about the matters subject to a vote — including director nominees, executive compensation, and proposals such as mergers. Choice A is incorrect because while governance documents may be relevant, the 1934 Act specifically requires a proxy statement with material disclosures, not copies of organizational documents.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Insider trading under Section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Burke, the CFO of Meridian Technology Corp., learns during a board meeting that Meridian will announce a major acquisition in two weeks. Before the announcement, Burke purchases 5,000 shares of Meridian stock through a personal brokerage account. Under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, Burke''s conduct most likely violates:', '["Section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5, which prohibit trading on material nonpublic information", "Section 11, which imposes liability for material misstatements in registration statements", "Section 18, which covers false statements in SEC filings", "Section 16(a), which requires reporting of insider transactions but does not prohibit them"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 10(b) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 and SEC Rule 10b-5, it is unlawful for any person to use material nonpublic information to trade securities. Burke''s purchase of Meridian shares based on knowledge of the unannounced acquisition constitutes insider trading. Choice D is incorrect because Section 16(a) requires insiders to report transactions but does not by itself prohibit trading — the substantive prohibition arises under Section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Agent's duty of care standard
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'An agent''s duty of care requires the agent to perform with what standard?', '["Absolute perfection in all tasks assigned by the principal", "Only the minimum effort needed to complete the task", "The competence and diligence expected of a reasonable person in similar circumstances", "The same level of skill as the most experienced professional in the field"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under agency law, an agent owes a duty of care requiring performance with the competence and diligence that a reasonable person would exercise in similar circumstances. This is an objective standard based on reasonableness, not perfection or minimal effort. Choice A is incorrect because the duty of care does not require absolute perfection — it requires only the exercise of reasonable competence and diligence.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — UCC Article 9 perfection and priority
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Lakeland Equipment lends $300,000 to Thompson Farms, taking a security interest in Thompson''s farm equipment. Lakeland files a UCC-1 financing statement with the appropriate state office. A month later, Second National Bank also obtains a security interest in the same equipment but does not file a financing statement. If Thompson defaults, which creditor has priority?', '["Second National Bank has priority because it extended credit more recently", "Both creditors share equally because both hold valid security interests", "Thompson''s unsecured creditors take priority over both secured parties", "Lakeland has priority because it perfected its security interest by filing a financing statement first"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under UCC Article 9, a creditor perfects a security interest typically by filing a UCC-1 financing statement with the appropriate filing office. Among competing security interests, a perfected interest takes priority over an unperfected one. Lakeland perfected by filing; Second National Bank did not. Choice A is incorrect because the timing of the credit extension is irrelevant — priority among secured creditors is determined by the order of perfection, not the order of the underlying transaction.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Form 10-K annual report
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, which periodic report must a registered company file annually with the SEC?', '["Form 10-K", "Form 10-Q", "Form 8-K", "Form S-1"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, registered companies must file Form 10-K annually, Form 10-Q quarterly, and Form 8-K for significant current events. Form S-1 is a registration statement under the Securities Act of 1933, not a periodic report. Choice B is incorrect because Form 10-Q is the quarterly report, not the annual filing required by the 1934 Act.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Intrastate offering exemption (Rule 147)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Sterling Realty, a company incorporated and doing business exclusively in Georgia, offers securities only to Georgia residents to fund a local development project. Sterling''s counsel advises that SEC registration is not required. Under the Securities Act of 1933, which exemption does this offering qualify for, and what happens if Sterling subsequently sells shares to an investor in Florida?', '["Regulation D Rule 506(b) exemption; the Florida sale has no effect on the exemption", "Rule 147 intrastate offering exemption; the Florida sale destroys the exemption for the entire offering", "Section 3(a)(11) exemption; the Florida sale is permitted as long as it occurs after the offering closes", "Regulation A exemption; selling to one out-of-state investor does not affect the exemption"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the Securities Act of 1933 and SEC Rule 147, the intrastate offering exemption applies when securities are offered and sold exclusively to residents of a single state by an issuer incorporated and doing business in that state. If even one sale is made to an out-of-state investor, the entire exemption is lost, potentially requiring registration of the full offering. Choice C is incorrect because Rule 147 requires all purchasers to be in-state residents — there is no post-closing exception for out-of-state sales.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Principal's duty of cooperation
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'A principal''s duty of cooperation in an agency relationship requires the principal to:', '["Ensure the agent achieves a profitable outcome on every transaction", "Provide the agent with unlimited authority to make all decisions", "Delegate all management responsibilities to the agent", "Not interfere with the agent''s proper performance of duties"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under agency law, the principal owes the agent a duty of cooperation, meaning the principal must not unreasonably interfere with the agent''s proper performance of assigned duties. This includes providing necessary information and access. Choice A is incorrect because the duty of cooperation does not require profitable results — it only requires the principal to refrain from obstructing the agent''s authorized activities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Merchant classification and heightened standards
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Romano, who owns a wholesale electronics distributorship and regularly sells consumer electronics, enters a verbal agreement with Jensen to sell 50 television sets at $400 each. Under UCC Article 2, Romano is classified as a merchant for this transaction. Which heightened standard applies to Romano because of this merchant classification?', '["Romano must provide a written warranty for every item sold", "Romano is exempt from the statute of frauds for goods under $500", "Romano is held to higher standards of good faith and fair dealing consistent with commercial practices of the trade", "Romano must register the transaction with the state commerce department"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under UCC Section 2-104, a merchant is a person who deals in goods of the kind involved in the transaction. UCC Article 2 imposes heightened standards on merchants, including the obligation of good faith and fair dealing consistent with commercial standards of the trade (UCC Section 2-103). Choice A is incorrect because while implied warranties such as merchantability arise automatically from merchant status, the UCC does not require a separate written warranty for every item sold.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Bankruptcy terminating agency by operation of law
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Gentry Corp. appoints Hayes as its exclusive purchasing agent with express authority to negotiate contracts with suppliers for a two-year term. Midway through the term, Gentry files for Chapter 7 bankruptcy. Hayes, unaware of the filing, signs a $200,000 supply contract with Omega Materials the following day. Omega also has no knowledge of the bankruptcy. Analyze the validity of the contract and the available remedies for Omega.', '["The contract is valid and enforceable because both Hayes and Omega acted without knowledge of the bankruptcy", "The agency terminated immediately upon Gentry''s bankruptcy filing, the contract is void, and Omega may seek damages only from Hayes for breach of implied warranty of authority", "The contract is voidable at Gentry''s election but remains binding if the bankruptcy trustee ratifies it", "The bankruptcy does not affect the agency because Hayes held a two-year contract that survives the filing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under agency law, the bankruptcy of the principal terminates the agency relationship by operation of law immediately and without notice. Hayes''s authority ceased when Gentry filed for Chapter 7, and the contract with Omega is void. However, Omega may recover damages from Hayes under the doctrine of implied warranty of authority — an agent who purports to act for a principal impliedly warrants that authority exists. Choice D is incorrect because even a fixed-term agency agreement does not survive the principal''s bankruptcy; termination by operation of law overrides contractual provisions.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — CPA common law liability to clients
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Harmon CPAs performs an annual audit for Lakeside Corp. under an engagement letter. Harmon negligently fails to detect a material overstatement of accounts receivable, causing Lakeside to overpay income taxes by $180,000. Under common law, on what basis can Lakeside sue Harmon?', '["Breach of contract and negligence arising from the engagement relationship", "Strict liability under the Securities Act of 1933", "Violation of Rule 10b-5 under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934", "Breach of fiduciary duty owed to Lakeside''s shareholders"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under common law, a CPA owes a duty of care to the client based on the contractual relationship established by the engagement letter. Lakeside can sue Harmon for both breach of contract (failure to perform the engagement as agreed) and negligence (failure to exercise reasonable professional care in conducting the audit). Choice B is incorrect because Section 11 strict liability applies only to misstatements in registration statements for public offerings, not to routine audits performed under private engagement letters.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — UCC open terms (price left open)
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Pearson calls Henderson on March 1, 2026, and says, "I want to buy 500 units of your Model X widgets — ship them by April 1." Henderson agrees and ships the goods, but the parties never discussed the price. A dispute later arises over the price. Under UCC Article 2, is the contract enforceable even though the price was never agreed upon?', '["No, because a contract cannot be formed without a definite price term", "No, because the absence of a price term indicates the parties did not intend to contract", "Yes, and the price is the reasonable market price at the time of delivery", "Yes, but only if both parties are merchants under UCC Article 2"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under UCC Section 2-305, a contract for the sale of goods can be formed even if the price is left open, provided the parties intended to make a contract. When the price is not settled, the price is a reasonable price at the time of delivery. Choice A is incorrect because UCC Article 2 departs from common law by allowing open terms — including price — as long as there is intent to contract and a reasonably certain basis for providing a remedy.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Preliminary prospectus (red herring) definition
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'During the waiting period of a securities registration under the Securities Act of 1933, the document distributed to prospective investors to gauge interest is called a:', '["Final prospectus", "Tombstone advertisement", "Blue sky filing", "Preliminary prospectus (red herring)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under the Securities Act of 1933, a preliminary prospectus — informally called a red herring because of the red legend printed on its cover — may be distributed during the waiting period between filing the registration statement and its effective date. It provides information about the offering but may not contain the final price. Choice A is incorrect because the final prospectus may only be delivered after the registration statement becomes effective, not during the waiting period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Illegality terminating agency by operation of law
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Which of the following events terminates an agency relationship by operation of law?', '["The agent requests a higher commission rate", "The subject matter of the agency becomes illegal", "The principal hires additional agents for the same task", "The agent takes a vacation without notifying the principal"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under agency law, an agency relationship terminates by operation of law when the subject matter of the agency becomes illegal or impossible. Other events causing termination by operation of law include the death or incapacity of either party and the bankruptcy of the principal. Choice A is incorrect because a request for higher compensation is a negotiation matter and does not automatically terminate the agency relationship.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Scienter definition under Rule 10b-5
(3, 'Agency and Regulation', 'Under Rule 10b-5 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, a plaintiff must prove scienter. Scienter refers to:', '["Intent to deceive, manipulate, or recklessly disregard the truth", "Proof that the defendant acted in good faith", "Evidence that the plaintiff suffered physical harm", "A showing that the defendant was unaware of the transaction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Rule 10b-5, scienter means the defendant acted with intent to deceive or manipulate, or with reckless disregard for the truth. This element distinguishes Rule 10b-5 from Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933, which does not require proof of intent. Choice B is incorrect because good faith is a defense relevant under Section 18 of the 1934 Act, not the standard of culpability that scienter represents under Rule 10b-5.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;